# The deadliest animal
subtitle: "Guess which animals kill the most people each year, and see why the deadliest are the ones that carry disease, not the ones with teeth."
thumb: band "It's not the shark" stamp "PLOT TWIST"

## The deadliest animal
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Malaria researchers would be proud.
    good: Most people never suspect the mosquito.
    poor: Films taught us to fear the wrong animals.
    none: 'Blame Jaws: everyone fears the shark.'
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Shark
      - Hippo
      - Crocodile
      - Dog
      - Snake
      - Human
      - Mosquito
    values:
      - 6
      - 500
      - 1000
      - 59000
      - 100000
      - 440000
      - 610000
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Deaths a year
    box: full
    icons:
      - shark
      - hippopotamus
      - crocodile
      - dog
      - snake
      - person standing
      - mosquito
sources:
    - id: isaf
      title: Yearly worldwide shark attack summary 2024
      authors: International Shark Attack File, Florida Museum of Natural History
      year: 2025
      url: https://www.floridamuseum.ufl.edu/shark-attacks/yearly-worldwide-summary/
      finding: about six unprovoked shark-bite deaths a year worldwide (2019–2023 average); four in 2024
    - id: gates
      title: The Deadliest Animal in the World
      authors: Bill Gates (Gates Notes)
      year: 2014
      finding: hippos about 500 and crocodiles about 1,000 human deaths a year
    - id: who_rabies
      title: Rabies (fact sheet)
      authors: World Health Organization
      year: 2024
      url: https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/rabies
      finding: rabies kills an estimated 59,000 people a year; dogs cause up to 99 % of human cases
    - id: who_snake
      title: Snakebite envenoming (fact sheet)
      authors: World Health Organization
      year: 2023
      url: https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/snakebite-envenoming
      finding: 81,000 to 138,000 people die of snakebite each year
    - id: unodc
      title: Global Study on Homicide 2023
      authors: UNODC
      year: 2023
      url: https://www.unodc.org/documents/data-and-analysis/gsh/2023/Global_study_on_homicide_2023_web.pdf
      finding: about 440,000 homicides a year on average 2019–2021 (458,000 in 2021), not counting deaths in war
    - id: who_malaria
      title: World Malaria Report 2025
      authors: World Health Organization
      year: 2025
      finding: an estimated 610,000 malaria deaths in 2024

    card title "The deadliest animal"

Which animal kills the most people? What we fear and what actually kills us turn out to be very different things. Here are four suspects.
    cards rank cites ["who_malaria","who_snake","who_rabies","isaf"] ends ["most","least"]
        * "Mosquito" icon mosquito
        * "Snake" icon snake
        * "Dog" icon dog
        * "Shark" icon shark

    ask "Rank them: which kills the most people each year?" on rank store r
        right: You got {r}.
        wrong: You got {r}.

    erase rank

Let's count the deaths in a year. Sharks, the villains of a hundred films: fewer than ten. The bar barely leaves the floor.
    draw axes bar_1

Hippos kill around five hundred, crocodiles about a thousand. Huge, fierce, and still almost invisible here.
    draw bar_2 bar_3

Dogs kill far more: nearly sixty thousand a year.
    draw bar_4

Snakes: around a hundred thousand, mostly farmers bitten far from any hospital with antivenom.
    draw bar_5

    ask "Now the mosquito. How many people does it kill a year? Drag its bar to your guess." on bar_7 store g relative true tolerance 0.25 default "200000"
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it is about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is about {g.true}. Most people guess far lower.

Six hundred thousand, from malaria alone. The smallest suspect on your list outkills every predator on this chart, many times over.
    highlight bar_7

Yet the bite itself is harmless. What kills is the malaria parasite, slipped into your blood with the mosquito's saliva as it drinks.
    highlight bar_7

And the dogs? Mostly not their teeth: rabies, a virus passed in a bite, and almost always fatal once symptoms begin.
    highlight bar_4

So the deadliest animals are not the strongest, but the ones that carry disease.
    highlight bar_7 bar_4

One bar is still missing, the runner-up: us. Murder alone kills about four hundred and forty thousand people a year, and wars add more.
    draw bar_6 delivery grave

So fear sharks if you must. But the animal worth fighting is the one you swat, which is why bed nets and malaria drugs save more lives than any shark net.
    highlight bar_1 effect circle
---
